<section class="content-quick-answer" aria-label="New Hampshire septic record search" data-search-intent-answer>
    <h2>Where can I find my New Hampshire septic approval or plan?</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Start online.</strong> Open <a href="https://www4.des.state.nh.us/SSBOneStop/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" data-track-click="official_source" data-track-source-context="nh_onestop_quick_start" data-track-target-type="official_source">NHDES Subsurface OneStop</a> and choose <strong>Subsurface Applications Status Query</strong>. NHDES directs most post-1986 approvals here.</li>
        <li><strong>Need the actual drawing?</strong> NHDES says electronic plans are available for most systems approved after 2015. For earlier plans or approvals from 1967–1986, use the <a href="https://onlineforms.nh.gov/?formtag=NHDES-W-05-010" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" data-track-click="official_source" data-track-source-context="nh_archive_quick_start" data-track-target-type="official_source">Subsurface Systems File and Archive Records Request</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Still missing?</strong> Ask the town whether it holds approval or plan copies. Save any written response; a missing online entry is not a condition assessment.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>These are the record-search routes described in <a href="https://www.des.nh.gov/sites/g/files/ehbemt341/files/documents/ssb-14.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NHDES's published guidance</a>, not a guarantee that a particular file is available. <a href="/septic-as-built-records/">Understand plans versus as-built records</a>.</p>
</section>
